import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How to Measure Your Feet for Shoes (Length, Width & Calf) | Attirel"
  description="Measure your foot length with the wall-and-ruler method, foot width at the ball, and calf circumference for boots, the same method the Attirel app uses."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-article px-5 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
      How to measure your feet for shoes that actually fit.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-smoke">
      Shoe sizes vary wildly between brands, but your foot’s length and width don’t.
      Measure once with a wall, a ruler, and a piece of paper, and you’ll never squint at a
      brand’s size chart again.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">1. Foot length: the wall method</h2>
    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 text-ink/85">
      <li>Place a piece of paper on a hard floor with its short edge touching a wall.</li>
      <li>Stand on the paper with your heel firmly against the wall, weight evenly on both feet.</li>
      <li>Mark the tip of your longest toe on the paper (it isn’t always the big toe).</li>
      <li>Step off and measure from the paper’s edge to your mark with a ruler.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-lg bg-white/70 p-3 text-sm text-smoke">
      <strong class="text-ink">Tip:</strong> Measure both feet and use the longer one, since most
      people’s feet differ slightly. And measure at the end of the day, when your feet are at
      their largest.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">2. Foot width: at the ball</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink/85">
      Stand on a piece of paper and trace your foot. Measure across the widest part, the ball
      of your foot. Most people measure between 3 and 4.5 inches.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-lg bg-white/70 p-3 text-sm text-smoke">
      <strong class="text-ink">Tip:</strong> Measure both feet and use the wider one. Foot width
      is what determines whether you need a narrow, standard, or wide shoe — a detail most size
      charts ignore entirely.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">3. Calf circumference (for boots)</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink/85">
      Wrap the tape around the widest part of your calf, keeping it parallel to the floor.
      This measurement is needed for knee-high and over-the-knee boots.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-lg bg-white/70 p-3 text-sm text-smoke">
      <strong class="text-ink">Tip:</strong> Measure while standing with your weight evenly
      distributed on both legs.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why this beats “I’m usually a 9”</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink/85">
      A US 9 in one brand can be a full half-size different in another, and width fits are
      rarely comparable at all. Your actual foot measurements are the one constant — which is
      exactly what Attirel uses to recommend the right size (and width) in every brand you shop.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-3xl bg-plum px-8 py-12 text-center text-white">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold">Feet measured? The rest is automatic.</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-white/80">
        Add your foot length and width to Attirel and get the right shoe size in every brand,
        including width fit.
      </p>
      <a
        href="https://attirel.ai"
        class="mt-6 inline-block rounded-full bg-white px-8 py-3 font-semibold text-plum transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
      >
        Get your shoe sizes at attirel.ai →
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
